    "progressSummary": "VERIFIED (0-axiom): Defined the Gaussian q-binomial qBinom over any CommRing by the q-Pascal recurrence; proved boundary laws, the bridge [n choose 1]_q = [n]_q, and the classical limit [n choose k]_1 = C(n,k). q-binomial theorem + box-partition model remain.",
    "builtItems": [
      "def qBinom q n k by q-Pascal recurrence over a CommRing (Proofs/GeometricSeriesOQ04OQ03.lean)",
      "qBinom_succ_succ: q-Pascal recurrence (definitional)",
      "qBinom_eq_zero_of_lt, qBinom_self, qBinom_zero_right/zero_succ: boundary laws",
      "qNat_succ': prepend recurrence [n+1]_q = q[n]_q + 1 for the parent quantum integer",
      "qBinom_one_right: [n choose 1]_q = [n]_q (bridge to parent)",
      "qBinom_at_one: [n choose k]_1 = C(n,k) (classical-limit counting interpretation)"
    ],
    "insights": [
      "Defining the Gaussian binomial BY the q-Pascal recurrence makes the recurrence and boundary base cases definitional (rfl), sidestepping division/integrality issues of the quotient formula in Z[q].",
      "Equation-compiler recursion with a fixed (q:R) prefix requires passing q explicitly in self-calls; otherwise the index is coerced into the q slot.",
      "The k=1 column of the q-Pascal triangle is exactly the parent's quantum integer [n]_q, tying the two entries together.",
      "At q=1 the Gaussian binomial degenerates to Nat.choose, giving the honest counting interpretation (k-subsets of an n-set)."
    ],
